- **C5. PCOMP-63 and B640-2: comment-only, and is every number true.** FAIL: any code byte changed in the s47 file; the transpiled output differs; a sentence your probe contradicts. (a) Transpile `main` (`d64efb8f`), `7b2b2386` and the head with the repo's own `typescript` (`ts.transpileModule(source, { compilerOptions: { removeComments: true, target: "ES2022", module: "ESNext" } })`, your own program), sha256 equal (RELAYED `e16b869111cbd9fc4ee858dd1d8df77839a9f8223cb6eee272a20e8b81a8f52d`); controls: one code character changed differs, one comment word changed is equal; `git diff -U0 e019ac3a -- <file>` shows 4 added lines starting ` *` and 0 removed (control: a planted code line is flagged); each added line at most 120 characters; ASCII; `prettier --check` and `eslint` rc 0 on both files. (b) **Probe in scratch copies of the s47 test file only, in `-probe`** (never the engine source): wrap `credentialShapes` inside `timeWhole` and `timePieces` (and nowhere else), the single `overSingleSplit` test selected with `-t`, ten attack inputs, the ratio each run prints (the failure text carries `x<ratio>`): **P-null** (harness baseline), **P-lin**, **P-fixed**, **P-win16k** (quadratic inside windows of 16,384), **P-win32k**, **P-win64k**, **P-whole** (the split is not dead) and, BRACKETING the sentence's stated edge, **P-win40k** (windows of 40,960 characters: predict whole/pieces about 2.5, GREEN) and **P-win57k** (57,344: predict about 3.5, RED). Compute the predicted ratio from your own cost model (about w/16,384 for w above the piece size, red above 3) and print it BEFORE each run. Each sentence of the comment is then marked: "a linear slowdown passes" (P-lin), "a fixed cost per call reads below 1 and is green" (P-fixed), "windows shorter than about three pieces (about 49,000)" (the bracket: the edge lies between the last green and the first red window; if 40,960 reads red or 57,344 reads green, the number is wrong), "16,384 or 32,768 read x1.0 or x2.0, 65,536 read x4.0" (your readings: within the distance of one timing sigma, the colour equal; say what the spread was). A probe whose timing is noisy under load is UNMEASURED, said so, never a pass: read the ratio's distance from the bound 3. (c) **Wording.** The comment does not state the piece size: say whether "three pieces" is exact for a reader (read `overSingleSplit` and print the lines: pieces of `ceil(whole.length / SPLIT_PARTS)` = 16,384) and whether the number would drift if `SPLIT_PARTS` or the string length changed (a Note). The builder dropped the "passed the old file too" clause: say whether any sentence now claims something about a former file (expected none).
- **C6. Names, NEW WORDS and suites.** FAIL: a lost run-time test name; a name added beyond the three new rows (and their describe); a changed or added sentence, title or error text that the builder's NEW WORDS section does not list; a red test; a count that contradicts the builder's. Static (your tokenizer over `it(`, `test(`, `describe(` titles in `apps` and `packages`): main (`d64efb8f`) vs the merge result as multisets, lost 0, added exactly 4 by the static tokenizer (3 `it` + 1 `describe`; predicted: the run-time list adds the 3 rows only, with the describe in their full names; the builder says "added 3": a difference of counting is a Note, not a contradiction, if you explain it) (control: one title dropped lost 1; a planted rename lost 1 and added 1). Run-time: the vitest JSON of the b116 file (3 at `-main`, 6 at the head) and s47 file (28 and 28), lost 0 added 3 / 0. **NEW WORDS by program:** take every added comment line, string literal, error text and title of the two files from the diff against `7b2b2386` and find each in the builder's STATUS section NEW WORDS (substring match on a distinctive token; control: a planted sentence is reported unlisted); a changed sentence not listed = a finding (Minor). Then the whole unit suite once at the merge result by the lineage's route (`unit_run.sh` copy; the SOW input exactly as Q-2 says, else NOT TESTED): **the expected count is the main base's own count plus 3 passed** (same file count, same skips); the builder's 311 files, 3106 passed + 1 skipped is RELAYED for the OLD base `e019ac3a` and is NOT the prediction here because main moved 15 files (among them `packages/api-contract/src/openapi.test.ts`); run the base (main `d64efb8f`, build + vitest, lint/format/typecheck cut and said so) and compare the two by test names: lost 0, added 3; say what your skip is (earlier lanes: `packages/renderers/test/measure-preview.test.ts`); lint, `format:check` and `typecheck` rc 0 over the merge result (the CI chain's steps; say what you cut). A red only under load and green re-run alone at load1 below 6 is a load artefact: record both.
- **C7. CodeQL, check-runs, merge.** FAIL: any check-run on the head not `completed` with `success` / `neutral` at the verdict; an open alert on the PR ref or the merge ref; the head not as stamped; an analysis missing at the verdict. Re-read the ruleset (both thresholds). `check-runs --paginate` at start, mid and verdict with the time (poll every 60 s while any is `in_progress`, at most 40 minutes beyond your last heavy job); `analyses?ref=refs/pull/90/head` (languages, `commit_sha` = the head, `results_count`, `error`) with `ref=refs/heads/main` as the row-count control; alerts with ALL states on the head ref, the merge ref and main (control: `analyses` of main returns rows; main's alerts are all fixed); the dismissed count at start and verdict equal (you never dismiss). A check `in_progress` or an analysis missing at the verdict: `CI pending: <names>`, the verdict is at most GO WITH FOLLOW-UPS and never a bare GO; a missing analysis is NOT scanned, never clean. What CodeQL could flag in the two files (no `fs`, no regular expression from input, no dynamic SQL, no `new RegExp(` with a non-literal; the new code is loops, a `performance.now()`, thrown strings and `task.timeout`): read-only grep over the added lines, categories printed with a positive control. **Merge in YOUR plain clone:** `git clone --no-hardlinks '<project>/2_Project_Files' '<project>/.tools/b659/repo'` (never `--shared`, never a worktree of the shared repo; prove the object link count 1 and the shared `.git/objects` mtime and `for-each-ref` count unchanged; push URL `DISABLED-no-push`; repo-local `core.sshCommand` equal to the shared repo's, read with `config --get`, never printed), ONE explicit fetch `'+refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main' '+refs/pull/90/head:refs/remotes/origin/pr90'` (the shared clone is stale and lacks `d64efb8f`), no `--prune`, no `--tags`, retry a `Permission denied (publickey)` 5 times about 10 s apart: `rev-list --left-right --count origin/main...origin/pr90` (expect `2 3` while main sits at `d64efb8f`: 2 only on main, 3 only on the PR), `git merge-tree --write-tree origin/main origin/pr90` rc 0, tree equal to `21b30f19e7634a06675e9361940e72fa044ec465` (GitHub's merge-ref tree) while main is unmoved (else explain by what main moved), its diff against main exactly the 2 paths and equal to `pulls/90/files` by GET; control: a scratch branch with one line of the b116 file changed at a base line conflicts (rc 1; if a first control does not conflict, report it and replace it). `pulls/90` `mergeable` / `mergeable_state` and `refs/pull/90/merge` (parents, tree) at start and verdict. **Order:** re-intersect NOW by GET (counts: #90 2 files, #86 2, #85 57; shared with #90 expected 0 each; controls: a planted shared path found, self-compare of #90 lists 2), plus every live `b6*` branch by `compare` (squash-merged history branches are listed, not stops), then say that the squash of #90 alone leaves #86 and #85 mergeable (merge-tree each on a scratch branch). If main moved again, use main at your read and say what moved.
